Task 2: Author Multi-Tier Terraform Configurations
Requirement 1: Design and implement two Terraform configurations â one representing a development tier and one representing a production tier â each in its own subdirectory under ~/cost-lab/. The development tier must include at minimum one EC2 instance, one RDS instance, and one S3 bucket. The production tier must include at minimum one Auto Scaling Group with a launch template, one Multi-AZ RDS instance with a subnet group across two private subnets, one Application Load Balancer, and one ElastiCache replication group. Both configurations must use the hashicorp/aws provider pinned to ~> 5.0, declare all tuneable values as input variables with defaults, and pass terraform init without errors.

The directory layout must be:

~/cost-lab/
  dev/
    main.tf
  prod/
    main.tf
Each main.tf must be entirely your own work. No templates are provided. Decide resource sizes, variable names, and tagging conventions yourself. The configurations do not need to be deployable against real AWS credentials; they only need to be syntactically valid and parseable by Infracost.

Requirement 2: Design and implement a third Terraform configuration in ~/cost-lab/optimized/ that targets the same functional role as the production tier but applies at least four distinct cost-reduction techniques. Techniques you may consider include (but are not limited to): replacing On-Demand EC2 with Spot requests, downsizing instance types, replacing a Multi-AZ RDS with a single-AZ instance, replacing an Application Load Balancer with a Network Load Balancer, enabling S3 Intelligent-Tiering, or reducing ElastiCache node count. Document each technique as an inline comment in main.tf on the line of the resource it affects.

Acceptance Criteria:

Running terraform init inside each of dev/, prod/, and optimized/ completes with the message Terraform has been successfully initialized! and no errors.
The optimized/main.tf file contains at least four inline comments, each beginning with the prefix # COST-OPT:, that each name a specific technique and the resource it applies to (for example: # COST-OPT: Spot Instance replaces On-Demand to reduce compute cost).
Task 3: Generate, Compare, and Interpret Cost Reports
Requirement 1: Using Infracost, generate a cost breakdown for each of the three configurations and save the results. For each configuration, produce both a JSON output file (for machine parsing) and a table-format text file (for human reading). All six output files must be stored under ~/cost-lab/reports/ with names that unambiguously identify the scenario and format (for example: dev-breakdown.json, dev-breakdown.txt).

Requirement 2: Write a shell script at ~/cost-lab/compare.sh that reads the three JSON breakdown files, extracts the totalMonthlyCost field from each using jq, prints a formatted three-row summary table to stdout showing scenario name and monthly cost, calculates the absolute dollar saving of the optimized tier versus the production tier, and writes the entire output to ~/cost-lab/reports/comparison-summary.txt. The script must exit with code 0 on success and print a descriptive error message (not a stack trace) if any of the three JSON files is missing.

Acceptance Criteria:

The directory ~/cost-lab/reports/ contains exactly six files matching the pattern <scenario>-breakdown.json and <scenario>-breakdown.txt for each of the three scenarios, and each .json file contains a totalMonthlyCost field with a numeric string value when inspected with jq -r '.totalMonthlyCost'.
Running bash ~/cost-lab/compare.sh exits with code 0, prints a table with three rows (one per scenario) each containing a dollar amount, prints a line of the form Optimized saves $X.XX/month vs Production, and creates ~/cost-lab/reports/comparison-summary.txt containing the same output.
Expected Outcomes
Three valid Terraform configurations exist representing development, production, and cost-optimized tiers, each with Infracost-generated JSON and table cost reports stored under a single reports directory.
A working shell script produces a three-scenario cost comparison table and quantifies the monthly saving achieved by the optimized tier relative to the production tier.
Conclusion
This lab required you to build a complete cost-estimation pipeline from a bare Ubuntu instance: installing the toolchain, authoring multi-resource Terraform configurations at different complexity levels, and writing automation to extract and compare cost data programmatically. The core skill is not memorizing Infracost flags but understanding how infrastructure design choices â instance type, redundancy level, load balancer type â translate directly into measurable monthly cost differences. Carry this cost-awareness habit into every infrastructure design decision you make.
